  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows some high-level stratiform cloud \ncover remaining over the area behind the cold front moving eastward \nout of the U.P. Skies are expected to clear out this evening \nthrough tonight. Winds will continue to shift northerly behind the \ncold front tonight and maintain strength at 15-20kts, making for \nbreezy overnight conditions. A combination of CAA and radiational \ncooling from mostly clear skies overnight is expected to result in \ntemperatures reaching the upper 30s in the eastern U.P. and along \nthe lakeshore. The central western U.P. will likely see lows at or \njust below freezing, resulting in freeze potential for the area. \n\nFriday is expected to be breezy and cool as a building area of \nsurface high pressure progresses eastward into the Great Lakes \nregion. 850mb temperatures are expected to drop below freezing early \nFriday morning, which could result in enough of a temperature \ndeficit with the lake surface to create scattered lake effect rain \nshowers for parts of the eastern U.P. Any precipitation that does \nfall is expected to remain brief and light. Winds reduce in strength \nand shift to southerly Friday night as the area of high pressure \nmoves over the area. With continued clear skies and limited mixing \ndue to weaker winds, Friday night's lows will be below freezing for \nnearly all areas inland from the lakeshore. The interior west could \nsee lows fall to the low 20s in some areas. Freeze headlines will \nlikely be needed.\n\nSoutherly winds continue Saturday, with highs in the low 60s. Cloud \ncover returns Saturday afternoon. Precipitation chances increase \nSaturday night as a cold front associated with a surface low in \nsouthern Ontario progresses across the U.P. Current 24hr QPF \nestimates from the NBM give probabilities of ~40% for greater than \none tenth of a chance of rain across the western and eastern U.P., \nwith an ~30% chance for the central U.P. Chances above 30% for a \nquarter of an inch of rain currently remain limited to the Keweenaw. \nOverall, most areas could see a light rain, however, significant \nrainfall amounts are unlikely. Northwesterly winds will pick up \nbehind the cold front. While inland areas will see winds around 10 \nmph, the NBM currently has chances for gale exceedance around 20-40% \nfor areas over water in the southeastern portion of Lake Superior. \nRain chances exit the area Sunday afternoon as surface high pressure \nmoves across the region. High pressure is expected to stick around, \nbringing dry conditions in the early workweek.",
